WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.411
- How did we get on evolution?

00:00:01.411 --> 00:00:02.244
This is not about about evolution.

00:00:02.244 --> 00:00:03.077
- Yes, exactly.

00:00:03.077 --> 00:00:03.923
You shouldn't be talking
about this at all.

00:00:03.923 --> 00:00:05.750
- When you attacked Mac's character!

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.350
- Yeah, like they did in the OJ trial

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:09.765
to that hero cop Mark Fuhrman.

00:00:09.765 --> 00:00:12.211
- (chuckles) No, not touching that one.

00:00:12.211 --> 00:00:14.794
(upbeat music)

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.570
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.330
Today we are covering another episode

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.360
of It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.193
This time,

00:00:24.193 --> 00:00:26.990
it is Reynolds Versus
Reynolds: The Cereal Defense.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:29.520
As always, be sure to comment
in the form of an objection

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.810
if you disagree with me,

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:31.643
and while you're there,

00:00:31.643 --> 00:00:34.180
let me know what show or
movie I should do next.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.490
And of course, stick around
until the end of the video

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:37.890
where I give this episode

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.930
of It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.090
a grade for legal realism.

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:43.280
So, without further ado,

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.573
let's dig in to Reynolds Versus Reynolds.

00:00:47.099 --> 00:00:48.170
[Charlie on Tape] You should pass three--

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:50.220
- This is stuck, Charlie, I need you here,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.110
this tape isn't working!

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:55.580
- [Announcer] This is KPFN, public radio.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.700
- [Woman] Today on Do
It Yourself Gardening--

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.950
- Ooh, more distracted driving,

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:01.453
with a bowl of cereal.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.250
Distracted driving is a big problem,

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.120
never drive while distracted.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.873
Always a bad idea.

00:01:07.873 --> 00:01:10.017
- Oh, Sandra, you dumb (bleep).

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:12.600
(car crashes)

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.930
Guys, so I'm sitting at a
red light, at a dead stop,

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.900
and Frank rams into me, outta nowhere,

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.810
and now he's saying he's not
gonna pay for the damages.

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.450
I mean, did you know
that the man cannot see?

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:23.740
- I can see, I got glasses,
I just need new lenses.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.800
- The lenses are what make
the glasses work, Frank.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.300
- If you are driving around
without the use of sight,

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.040
then that is completely irresponsible

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.520
and you need to pay for the damages.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.720
- Oh, man, this is actually incredible,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.970
because this debate hearkens back

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.730
to one of the seminal cases in law school,

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.520
specifically the case of
Hadley versus Baxendale,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.110
where someone was supposed to
deliver a crankshaft to a mill

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.500
and the mill owner sued
the delivery person

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.140
not just for the exact
damages that occurred,

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.440
but for the loss of income

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.350
that occurred for the days afterwards

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.330
for failing to receive the crankshaft

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.920
which was necessary for the mill.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.670
The Court of Exchequer in England,

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.540
which is equivalent to the Supreme Court

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.820
in the United States,

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:07.070
held that the delivery person
couldn't be held responsible

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.820
for the consequential
damages that occurred

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.040
as a result of failing to
meet their delivery deadline.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.760
And what they held is that
you're only responsible

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.850
for the damages that are
reasonably foreseeable

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.880
as a result of your negligence.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.210
So I think what's going to happen

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.800
throughout this entire episode

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:23.950
is it's going to be a question

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.070
about whether it was
reasonably foreseeable

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:28.570
for Frank to have caused these damages

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.800
to the interior of Dennis's car,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.560
and it's not an easy question.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.520
It's hilarious to me that they set this up

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.070
basically as a hypothetical
that you would have to deal with

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:38.540
as a first-year law student.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:39.410
And it's also funny

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:42.190
considering how bad the last legal episode

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:44.340
of It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia was.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.764
- Objection, she's not a cat, Your Honor.

00:02:46.764 --> 00:02:48.000
(Maureen hisses)

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.500
- This is, I think, gonna
get a much higher grade.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.010
- He was eating a bowl
of cereal when I hit him,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:54.740
and it spilled all over the interior,

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:57.670
and those are the only damages to the car.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.743
- You were eating a bowl of cereal?

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:02.413
- Yes.

00:03:02.413 --> 00:03:03.246
- While you were driving?

00:03:03.246 --> 00:03:06.460
- Yes, it's not that crazy,
that's not what this is about.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.809
- So the only damages were interior.

00:03:08.809 --> 00:03:10.410
- You know what, I don't have
to justify myself to you,

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:11.352
you know what I'm gonna do?

00:03:11.352 --> 00:03:12.185
I'm gonna take you to court!

00:03:12.185 --> 00:03:13.120
- Fine.
- No, no, hold on a second,

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:14.620
nobody needs to go to court here,

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:16.670
there's no reason we can't
settle this ourselves

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:17.920
like civilized adults.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.990
- No, no, we can't, Dee, so
I'm gonna take him to court,

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.330
I'm gonna sue him, and I'm gonna win.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.060
- Dennis, you don't wanna
go anywhere near a court,

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:26.770
how many bench warrants for
sexual misconduct do you have?

00:03:26.770 --> 00:03:28.790
- Would there be bench
warrants for sexual misconduct?

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:29.840
I don't think that's a thing.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:33.470
- First off, who is the
defendant in this matter?

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:34.450
- That's not a statement.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:37.120
- Alright, well, irregardless,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.230
I would like to know, as Frank's lawyer,

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.290
who I am persecuting
or who I am defending.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:42.160
- Yeah, prosecuting.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.790
- Okay, let me just nip this whole thing

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:44.640
in the bud right now,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:46.200
stop treating this like it's a trial

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.320
and like this is a courtroom.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:48.160
- Yeah, but the tables.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.590
- Yeah, but I was against that too.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.470
Okay, let's just present
the facts to each other,

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.790
and then let's decide as a
majority who's in the right.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.330
That being said, Frank
would be the defendant.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:56.820
- I'm not the defendant.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.570
No, that implies that I am guilty.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:03:59.403
- He's right,

00:03:59.403 --> 00:04:00.520
if anyone's ever accused of anything,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:01.910
I automatically assume they're guilty.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:02.743
- [Charlie] You do, right?

00:04:02.743 --> 00:04:03.576
- I do.
- Yeah, you do.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.380
- It's unfortunate that a
lotta people feel that way,

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:06.800
if someone is accused of something,

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.000
that they have a presumption of guilt.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.530
That should not be the case,

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:14.930
it's the prosecutor or the
plaintiff that bears the burden.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:15.763
That being said,

00:04:15.763 --> 00:04:18.650
here, clearly, Frank
would be the defendant,

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:19.730
he's the one that was driving,

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:21.260
he's the one that caused

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.710
the things that are
potentially damages here.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:26.740
There's no debate here,
Frank would be the defendant.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.070
- I'm finished.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:29.720
- Where have you been
for the last three hours?

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.720
- I have been making this.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:33.850
- What the hell is that?

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:35.020
- It's the Trial Meter.

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.780
It shows how we each feel
throughout the trial.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.100
Yeah, I made little gavels
with our names on it,

00:04:40.100 --> 00:04:41.410
and we can move them accordingly,

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.243
and at the end,

00:04:42.243 --> 00:04:44.980
whoever has the most
gavels on their side wins.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:47.500
- It took you three hours to make that?

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.333
- Yeah.

00:04:48.333 --> 00:04:50.180
- Mac, let me tell you something,

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:53.080
nobody is gonna wanna use
that, because it's so stupid.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.900
We are dealing with a serious issue here.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.720
- As ridiculous as this sort
of cartoon interpretation

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.850
of which way all the different
people are leaning is,

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.600
it's actually not a bad way

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.300
of deciding who, in this
sort of quasi-arbitration,

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:09.720
is going to win here.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.200
Obviously the parties are
gonna vote for themselves,

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.830
but ironically, in an arbitration,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.110
which this sort of is,

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:18.200
you would often have a three-judge panel.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:21.400
Sometimes you would have only
one judge, or arbitrator,

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.970
but often, you would have
a three-arbitrator panel

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.350
that's made up of former judges.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.320
And guess what, the majority wins,

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.410
so if you're able to convince

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:30.940
two out of the three arbitrators,

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.760
then you would win your
case in arbitration.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.010
So it's kind of a funny version

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:36.140
of what happens in real life.

00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.730
- Uh, I would like a five
with my client please.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:39.633
- Are you gonna want a five, Counselor?

00:05:39.633 --> 00:05:40.642
- I'd like to take a five, yes.

00:05:40.642 --> 00:05:41.520
- Alright, I'll allow it.
- Stop talking

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.840
like this is a (bleep) courtroom, please.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:44.700
- Oh, I'm the judge.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:45.570
- No, you're not.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:46.460
- Can I be the bailiff?

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:47.400
- You are not anything!

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:48.233
- I'll allow it.

00:05:48.233 --> 00:05:49.066
- [Dennis] No one is anything!

00:05:49.066 --> 00:05:49.899
- Yeah, he's an arbitrator.

00:05:49.899 --> 00:05:52.460
- I will smash everybody's
eyes out of their sockets!

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:54.730
- And that is one of the reasons why

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:56.690
you never want to represent yourself,

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.420
you have too much skin in the game,

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:00.080
it's too hard to be objective,

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.700
and it is too easy to have
an outburst like that.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:05.870
Dennis should not be representing
himself in this matter,

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:09.645
he should really be relying
on his punitive counselor Dee.

00:06:09.645 --> 00:06:13.080
- I would like to add
into evidence article one.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.940
Mac, will you please read this document?

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:17.407
- Mm-hmm.

00:06:17.407 --> 00:06:19.867
"By the power of the
Commonwealth of Pennsylvania,

00:06:19.867 --> 00:06:23.167
"Reed Mental Institution
hereby decrees Frank Reynolds

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:26.909
"to not have donkey brains."

00:06:26.909 --> 00:06:27.742
(intense music)

00:06:27.742 --> 00:06:28.575
- What?
- What?

00:06:28.575 --> 00:06:29.662
- That is an official document

00:06:29.662 --> 00:06:32.230
that says donkey brain on it?
- What is that?

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.380
- Alright, so there's a
lotta stuff going on here.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:38.110
Interestingly, Charlie is not
doing a terrible, terrible job

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:40.010
of laying the proper
foundation that he needs

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:41.480
to enter this thing into evidence.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:44.310
And in fact, like few legal shows,

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:46.100
he actually says that he wants to enter

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:48.320
this particular document into evidence.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.290
You actually don't see
that all that often.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:52.760
He missed a few steps.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.950
Number one, this document is hearsay,

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:57.430
regardless of where it came from,

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:58.320
because he's trying to use it

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.800
for the truth the matter asserted,

00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:04.159
that Frank, in fact, does
not have donkey brains.

00:07:04.159 --> 00:07:06.580
So he has hearsay problems to deal with,

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:09.640
he has to establish that there
is some hearsay exception,

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.360
but he has gone part of
the way towards there.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.320
He has established that Frank received

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:16.860
this document at some point,

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:20.500
and he has some basis to
say what this document is.

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.090
He still needs to show

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:25.600
that it was a business record
of the mental institution,

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:26.590
and the only way to do that

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:29.960
is to get a custodian of
the mental institution.

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:32.500
So he certainly hasn't
laid all of the foundation,

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.700
but he is part of the way there,

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:37.410
which is more than I can say
for a lot of legal TV shows,

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:38.280
that's pretty funny.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.150
- Frank, would you like to
clear this up for everybody?

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:41.770
- Well,

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:44.920
all the kids in the neighborhood
knew I got sent upstate,

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:47.580
so they started calling
me Frankie Donkey Brains,

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.000
and it was very traumatic,

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.590
so I got my mommy to drive
me back up to the loony bin,

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:55.200
where they signed this
official certificate

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.710
exonerating me of all donkey brains.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.930
- Yeah, great, what does this
have to do with anything?

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.310
- Yeah, that is a good objection.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:04.890
What Charlie is trying to do here

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:08.140
is to enter character
evidence on behalf of Frank.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:11.050
He's trying to say that
Frank has a character

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:12.790
for lack of donkey brains,

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:14.070
which is a ridiculous sentence

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.460
for me to have to say right now,

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.020
but anyway, that's what he's trying to do.

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:18.580
And what he's trying to show

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:21.650
is that on the day in
question, of the accident,

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:22.980
he's saying that Frank

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:25.520
was not acting with
(chuckles) donkey brains,

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.040
and therefore could
not have been negligent

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.370
in driving his car.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:31.260
So at base, this is a really good example

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:33.610
of why character evidence
should not be allowed.

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:36.170
Just because Frank might
have had a character

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:38.480
for lack of donkey brains
when he was a child

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:41.090
doesn't mean he didn't act negligently

00:08:41.090 --> 00:08:44.658
when he ran into Dennis in
this particular instance.

00:08:44.658 --> 00:08:47.503
- Do you have any such certificate?

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:49.793
- What? (scoffs)

00:08:49.793 --> 00:08:53.640
- Well, we don't want a donkey
on the road eating cereal.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.620
We know Frank's not a donkey,

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.560
how do we know you're
not a donkey-brained man?

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:01.393
- Yep,

00:09:01.393 --> 00:09:03.780
a good example of why character
evidence is a bad idea.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.270
You're trying to prove a negative here,

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:07.030
to show that you weren't a donkey brain

00:09:07.030 --> 00:09:09.810
when you were the victim of this accident.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.580
The fact that Frank has the
certificate means nothing,

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.370
and this is a speciesist argument

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:15.430
that Charlie is using here,

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.060
it shouldn't hold any water.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.840
- Why would I have a certificate?

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.360
- You don't have a certificate?

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.120
- Oh, no, no, no, no, the
burden of proof is not on me.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:24.730
- The defendant will answer the question.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:26.850
- The defendant? I'm not the defendant!

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.430
- Just answer the question.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:30.160
- Those are two contradictory statements.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.230
He's not the defendant, that's accurate,

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:34.370
but he does have the burden of proof here.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:37.040
He has to prove that it
is more likely than not

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:38.190
that Frank was negligent

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.840
and that the damages that he suffered

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.572
are attributable to that negligence.

00:09:42.572 --> 00:09:45.660
(upbeat music)

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:49.190
- Frank, would you like a glass of wine?

00:09:49.190 --> 00:09:50.310
- Yeah, alright.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:51.830
- Huh, why not have a glass of wine?

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:53.040
I mean, after all...

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:55.750
Oh. (chuckles)

00:09:55.750 --> 00:09:56.857
- Hey, hey, hey, you can't do that!

00:09:56.857 --> 00:09:58.250
- Oh, I'm so sorry.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:09:59.730
Whoopsie, whoopsie,

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.520
see, I bashed into Frank
while he was at a dead stop,

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.440
that's completely my fault, Frank.

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:07.650
I will pay for all the
damages to your shirt.

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.160
Case closed!

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:12.030
- Oh, well, when you put
it like that, you got me.

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:13.510
You know what, I'm on Dennis's side now,

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:15.330
he just convinced me.

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:17.260
- Alright, I think Charlie's
being facetious here,

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:18.950
but Dennis's argument here

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:21.000
really goes to the heart of the matter.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.320
He is trying to establish that
the damages that he suffered,

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.220
the liquid coming out of his cereal bowl

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:29.710
and ruining the interior of his car,

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:32.600
are analogous to running into Frank

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:35.000
and spilling the red wine onto his shirt.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.840
This is what lawyers do all the time,

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:38.230
we take case law,

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:40.630
we make analogies to prior cases

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:42.600
that involve a similar set of facts,

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.890
and we say that the facts at issue

00:10:44.890 --> 00:10:47.430
are similar to the facts
that have gone before.

00:10:47.430 --> 00:10:48.263
And here,

00:10:48.263 --> 00:10:51.030
he is trying to make an
analogy between the liquid,

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.930
the milk, in a cereal bowl

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:55.840
to the liquid, the wine,
that was in the wine glass.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:58.390
So really good job by
Dennis making an argument

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.320
that goes to the heart of the matter here,

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:02.210
whether it was reasonably foreseeable

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.190
for Frank to have caused the damages

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:06.970
to the inside of Dennis's car.

00:11:06.970 --> 00:11:09.630
- It occurs to me that Frank

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:11.960
assumed responsibility
for a potential spill

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.270
the second he took the wine.

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:15.490
- Yeah, he's right, I knew the risk.

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.170
- So in this circumstance,

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.180
it would appear that Frank is at fault,

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:21.530
and therefore in the
car, Dennis is at fault,

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:24.030
I am on Frank's side, case closed!

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.740
- That's another good argument.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:28.180
There is a doctrine
called Assumption of Risk

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:29.320
in negligence law.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:30.153
Sometimes,

00:11:30.153 --> 00:11:33.380
if you do an inherently-dangerous
act as the victim,

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:37.440
and you are damaged as a result
of both someone's negligence

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.370
and your own actions,

00:11:39.370 --> 00:11:41.280
the law imputes an assumption of risk,

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:44.810
and it is an affirmative defense
to a claim of negligence.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:46.280
It's a doctrine that comes into play

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:48.320
when a victim knowingly and voluntarily

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.460
takes on the risks associated
with a certain activity.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.190
So, for example, if you were trespassing

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:54.737
and you saw a sign that said

00:11:54.737 --> 00:11:57.110
"Enter at Your Own Risk,
Dangerous Condition"

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.920
or "Caution: Guard Dog",

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:01.220
you might be subject

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:03.910
to the affirmative defense
of assumption of risk

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:06.610
because you knowingly took on the risks

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:09.090
associated with that particular behavior.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:11.180
So this is a great
argument for Mac to make,

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:14.760
this is a defense that gets
used in courts all the time.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:17.520
I don't think it applies in
this particular situation,

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.267
but let's see what the other arguments are

00:12:19.267 --> 00:12:20.660
and see how it comes out.

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:22.350
- I'm gonna ask you one more time,

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:24.050
do you or do you not believe

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:28.320
that you can create a
superhuman race of strongmen

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:30.543
through genetic mutation and evolution?

00:12:30.543 --> 00:12:33.210
(intense music)

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.170
- Uh, no. (chuckles)

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:40.440
That's ridiculous, Dee,
what are you talking about?

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:41.534
- What are you talking about?

00:12:41.534 --> 00:12:42.394
You were just...

00:12:42.394 --> 00:12:43.520
You were just telling Charlie this morning

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.370
all about the, "Oh, have you seen X-Men?"

00:12:45.370 --> 00:12:46.203
and "my seed", and--

00:12:46.203 --> 00:12:48.170
- No, I was joking with
Charlie. (chuckles)

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:49.880
That could never happen
in the real world, Dee,

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:50.970
that's like a comic book thing.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:51.860
- He's credible.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:52.693
- Yeah, he's credible.

00:12:52.693 --> 00:12:54.533
Alright, we won this case.
- I win this case.

00:12:54.533 --> 00:12:55.853
It's an open-shut case.

00:12:55.853 --> 00:12:56.686
- [Dee] Shit, Dennis, that was...

00:12:56.686 --> 00:12:59.290
- No, no, wait, wait, wait,
wait, wait, wait a minute.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:01.940
Mac, why don't you believe that?

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:02.820
- Huh?

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:04.940
- Well, I'm just wondering
why don't you believe

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.680
that you could pass down a gene

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:11.020
that would eventually evolve
into a race of supermen?

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:11.853
Why?

00:13:11.853 --> 00:13:13.130
- I like where Dennis is going here.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:14.360
This is one of the reasons

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:17.070
why you should never lie in court.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:18.390
It's because a good lawyer

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.740
will be able to suss out your lies

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:23.360
and show that you are
not a credible person.

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:24.250
When you're on the stand,

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:25.980
often, credibility is important,

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:27.640
not in the sense that
they're showing here,

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:29.330
but in the sense that
they are trying to show

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:31.270
that this witness is not credible

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:32.490
because they have lied,

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:35.610
and usually your
character for truthfulness

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:37.540
is usually at issue,

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:39.990
which is separate from
your general character

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.580
and certainly separate from
whether you had a character

00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:45.660
to engage in some sort of
negligence on the day in question.

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:48.910
So, whether they take you
down the logical route

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.410
of all the things that come
as a result of your lie

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:52.670
or whether they show you something

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:55.010
that contradicts your prior testimony,

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:56.450
so whether it's a deposition

00:13:56.450 --> 00:13:58.470
or perhaps an email that you wrote

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:00.380
that contradicts what
you're saying on the stand,

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:03.080
a good lawyer will be able
to tell when you're lying,

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:04.800
and they will ram it down your throat

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:05.810
and show everyone

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:07.670
that you are no longer
credible as a witness,

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:10.550
which I think is about
to happen right here.

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:11.750
- That's a silly question, (chuckles)

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.714
because evolution
doesn't exist, of course.

00:14:13.714 --> 00:14:14.547
(upbeat music)

00:14:14.547 --> 00:14:15.380
- [Dennis] I'm sorry?

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:16.810
- Oh, could you repeat
that again, for the room?

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:19.160
- Because evolution is
(bleep), it's not real.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:20.963
- God (bleep), recess!

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:26.410
- Yeah, or they'll just catch
you in some other absurdity

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:28.990
and ruin your credibility that way,

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:30.190
that can happen too.

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:31.490
Okay, I know at this point

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:32.660
a lot of people are going to say

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:34.200
this is completely unrealistic,

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.920
this is ridiculous, they're
having a fake trial in a bar,

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:39.130
so let's talk about what's
generally happening here,

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:42.570
and let's talk specifically
about legal arbitrations.

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.848
Let's think like a lawyer.

00:14:44.848 --> 00:14:47.431
(upbeat music)

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:51.430
Now, an arbitration is like a court

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.220
and a trial that happens in a court,

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:55.660
except it's all done by private contract.

00:14:55.660 --> 00:14:58.230
So, two parties get into a dispute,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.650
and rather then take
their dispute to court,

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:03.990
they either decide, at that
time, to do an arbitration

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:05.960
or they have a prior contract

00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:08.480
that requires them to go to arbitration.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.270
Arbitration can have some advantages,

00:15:10.270 --> 00:15:12.190
it can be much, much faster than a court,

00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:16.100
which a court trial can
often take years to happen,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:17.920
but it does have the disadvantage

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:20.130
of requiring the parties themselves

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:22.960
to pay the arbitrator,
or arbitrators, plural,

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:24.150
if there's more than one,

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:26.152
so it can often be more expensive

00:15:26.152 --> 00:15:28.750
than a courtroom proceeding.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:30.620
If you wanna know more about arbitrations,

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:33.830
I actually did a whole video
about the arbitration provision

00:15:33.830 --> 00:15:36.320
in the Discord and Better
Health terms of service,

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.960
I'll put a link to that down below.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:40.330
Believe it or not, when
you go to arbitration,

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:43.060
all of the rules are set
by you and your opponent,

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.230
you have to agree.

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:46.480
Arbitration is a creature of contract,

00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:49.500
and a contract requires mutual agreement.

00:15:49.500 --> 00:15:51.280
So if you agree to arbitration,

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:54.060
you and the other party
have to agree to the terms

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.460
by which that arbitration will proceed.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.700
So here, the parties, Dennis and Frank,

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.400
have basically agreed
to arbitrate their case

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:02.940
and avoid going to court,

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:04.780
even though this is sort
of the classic thing

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.010
that get litigated in court,

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:08.080
negligence in a car accident,

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:10.890
and they have sort of agreed
to be bound by the outcome

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:13.120
of what their three friends

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.520
think about this particular case.

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:16.420
So, believe it or not,

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:20.810
this is not that dissimilar
from an actual arbitration.

00:16:20.810 --> 00:16:22.430
It's a little bit crazier,

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:23.700
but you could agree

00:16:23.700 --> 00:16:26.090
to sort of forego the rules
of evidence if you wanted to

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:28.750
so that all of the
testimony that's coming in

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:30.660
would come in in arbitration too,

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:32.330
and you could agree to be bound by

00:16:32.330 --> 00:16:35.680
what your three friends think
about the particular case.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:39.140
- These were all the smartest
scientists on the planet,

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:44.133
only problem is, they kept
being wrong, sometimes.

00:16:44.133 --> 00:16:45.380
(intense music)
(Devin chuckles)

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:47.560
- This is insane, you fool!

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.190
- This is getting dangerously close

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:50.660
to the Chewbacca defense.

00:16:50.660 --> 00:16:53.290
- Why would a Wookie, an
eight-foot tall Wookie,

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:56.270
want to live on Endor with a
bunch of two-foot tall Ewoks?

00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:58.470
That does not make sense!

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:00.430
But more important, you
have to ask yourself,

00:17:00.430 --> 00:17:03.363
what does this have to do with this case?

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:05.463
Nothing.

00:17:05.463 --> 00:17:08.620
- Which, ironically, since
that came out in South Park,

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:10.450
is a term that lawyers use.

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:13.200
When we talk about arguments
that are completely ridiculous

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:15.580
and that have no bearing
on the particular case,

00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:17.540
except for some speciesist reasoning,

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:20.990
we actually do refer to things
as the Chewbacca defense.

00:17:20.990 --> 00:17:23.619
- Have you seen these fossil records?

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:24.452
(intense music)

00:17:24.452 --> 00:17:25.340
- Have I...

00:17:25.340 --> 00:17:26.250
Huh?

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:29.336
- Have you pored through the
data yourself, the numbers?

00:17:29.336 --> 00:17:31.480
- Oh, this thing is getting outta hand.

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:33.670
- So let me get this
straight, Mr. Reynolds,

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:35.350
you get your information from a book

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:37.880
written by men you've never met,

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:39.430
and you take their words as truth

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:41.060
based on a willingness to believe,

00:17:41.060 --> 00:17:42.820
a desire to accept,

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:46.907
a leap of, oof, dare I
say it, (scoffs) faith?

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.140
- I think the writers of
the show had a lot of fun

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:53.490
coming up with as many logical fallacies

00:17:53.490 --> 00:17:57.150
as they possibly could
cram into 22 minutes.

00:17:57.150 --> 00:18:00.040
None of this testimony is
relevant, we've gone far afield,

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:02.130
I hope we get back to
the actual trial at hand,

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:04.150
'cause there are some really
interesting legal issues

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:05.300
to discuss here:

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:08.870
reasonability, foreseeability, negligence,

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:10.200
this is fun stuff.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:11.340
Or at least it was.

00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:12.600
- Come on, look,

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:14.760
I mean, don't even know how I'm
supposed to respond to that,

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:16.336
like...

00:18:16.336 --> 00:18:17.840
Ah, come on.

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:20.040
That's a false equivalency.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:21.157
- Just answer the question, Mr. Reynolds.

00:18:21.157 --> 00:18:22.493
- (chuckles) False equivalency.

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:26.083
- Sure, yeah, okay.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:29.540
- I rest my case.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:30.373
- Wow!
(upbeat music)

00:18:30.373 --> 00:18:31.206
Well, that got me.

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:33.473
Frank, you want me to...

00:18:33.473 --> 00:18:34.306
- Put me over too.

00:18:34.306 --> 00:18:35.139
- Yeah, alright.

00:18:35.139 --> 00:18:35.972
- What?

00:18:35.972 --> 00:18:36.805
- Well, we're going on the fence,

00:18:36.805 --> 00:18:37.824
I mean, that's a shadow of a doubt.

00:18:37.824 --> 00:18:38.850
- You actually don't believe
- (chuckles) Shadow of doubt.

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:40.110
- in evolution anymore?

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:42.170
- I don't know, he created
a reasonable doubt,

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:45.083
he makes you sound like a
stupid, uh, science bitch.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:49.020
- So Charlie here is
getting the standard wrong,

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:52.770
even though none of this is
relevant to the case at hand,

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:54.960
he's talking about the
criminal law standard,

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:56.250
which is beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.300
You cannot have any reasonable doubts

00:18:58.300 --> 00:19:00.690
in order to find someone
guilty in criminal court.

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:03.960
It is the highest burden in all of law.

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:06.110
Whereas when you're talking
about a civil lawsuit,

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:08.610
you're only talking about a
preponderance of the evidence,

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:10.410
something that is more likely than not.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:14.790
In other words, if 50% is
exactly the same on either side,

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.870
all you have to be is 51% sure,

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:19.760
then the plaintiff has met their burden,

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:22.290
just 51% more likely than not

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.140
that the defendant committed
the acts accused of

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:30.100
and that that forms the basis
of the liability at issue.

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:31.500
- Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait,

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:33.270
what, what are we even talking about?

00:19:33.270 --> 00:19:34.722
How did we get on evolution?

00:19:34.722 --> 00:19:35.891
This is not about evolution.
- Yes, exactly,

00:19:35.891 --> 00:19:36.983
you shouldn't be talking
about this at all.

00:19:36.983 --> 00:19:38.930
- When you attacked Mac's character!

00:19:38.930 --> 00:19:40.540
- Yeah, like they did in the OJ trial

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:42.811
to that hero cop Mark Fuhrman.

00:19:42.811 --> 00:19:45.250
- (chuckles) No, not touching that one.

00:19:45.250 --> 00:19:47.150
- Okay, let's just get back on track here,

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:48.958
I would like to make my closing arguments.

00:19:48.958 --> 00:19:51.760
- No, no, no, no, no, no,
this has gone on long enough.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:52.593
And by the way, Dee,

00:19:52.593 --> 00:19:55.223
why the hell do you give a
(bleep) about any of this anyway?

00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:58.040
- You don't know?

00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.210
You can't think of why

00:19:59.210 --> 00:20:01.480
I might like to set a
precedent of responsibility

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:03.380
for when someone's car gets destroyed?

00:20:04.560 --> 00:20:05.393
- No.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:07.280
- So, ironically,

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.410
arbitrations don't really
set legal precedent.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:11.690
When you take a case to court

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:14.320
and that's adjudicated by
either the jury or the judge,

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:16.700
it really doesn't have
any precedential effect

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:20.270
until one of the parties
takes that case up on appeal.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:23.600
So, Dee won't get any precedent
out of this arbitration

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:25.380
because it's not taking place in court

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.292
and has not taken place in
front of the Court of Appeals.

00:20:28.292 --> 00:20:29.260
(upbeat music)
- Okay, here's the deal,

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.050
Dennis, if you can drive
to your apartment and back

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:32.660
without spilling a single drop,

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:33.800
Dee, your precedent is set,

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.170
and Frank, you pay for everything.

00:20:35.170 --> 00:20:36.003
- Fine.

00:20:36.003 --> 00:20:36.836
- Good.

00:20:36.836 --> 00:20:38.290
- Okay, and Frank, if you could
drive to our place and back

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:39.230
without getting in an accident,

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:40.170
with me giving you directions,

00:20:40.170 --> 00:20:41.440
you can continue to drive

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:42.760
as long as I'm in the car with you.

00:20:42.760 --> 00:20:43.593
- Deal.

00:20:43.593 --> 00:20:44.426
- Alright.

00:20:44.426 --> 00:20:46.006
- On your mark, get set, go!

00:20:46.006 --> 00:20:46.920
- I got it.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:48.350
- This is amazing,

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:51.380
this is a great way to resolve
this particular dispute,

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:54.270
the parties are agreeing to the facts.

00:20:54.270 --> 00:20:56.400
They're basically
creating an objective test

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.570
to determine whether Frank was negligent

00:20:58.570 --> 00:20:59.950
in the first instance

00:20:59.950 --> 00:21:04.270
based on his lack of correct
lenses in his glasses,

00:21:04.270 --> 00:21:05.630
and they're all agreeing to these terms,

00:21:05.630 --> 00:21:08.890
so they're setting a metric
by which everyone can agree.

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:09.723
This is amazing,

00:21:09.723 --> 00:21:11.190
I can't believe this is coming as a result

00:21:11.190 --> 00:21:12.700
of this stupid show.

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:13.533
- [Frank] Alright, here we go.

00:21:13.533 --> 00:21:14.537
- We're gonna make a left up there.

00:21:14.537 --> 00:21:15.620
- Make a what?
- Uh-huh.

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:16.453
- Go left.

00:21:16.453 --> 00:21:17.485
- Make a left?

00:21:17.485 --> 00:21:18.318
- Right.
- Right?

00:21:18.318 --> 00:21:19.449
- Oh no, I think I know
where this is going.

00:21:19.449 --> 00:21:20.282
(yelling and arguing)

00:21:20.282 --> 00:21:21.115
- You make up your mind!

00:21:21.115 --> 00:21:22.578
Deandra, shut up!

00:21:22.578 --> 00:21:23.703
- (chuckles) Oh no.

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:27.142
(car crashes)

00:21:27.142 --> 00:21:28.060
(Mac groans)

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:29.560
No!

00:21:29.560 --> 00:21:31.580
There's no resolution!

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:34.254
- That is insane, how do you hit me again?

00:21:34.254 --> 00:21:35.370
- It was Deandra's fault!

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:36.351
She gave me the wrong directions.

00:21:36.351 --> 00:21:37.184
- Guys, it's actually pretty cut and dry,

00:21:37.184 --> 00:21:38.540
- This way, that way, up.
- it was Dee's fault,

00:21:38.540 --> 00:21:40.201
I was trying to give directions
and she interrupted and--

00:21:40.201 --> 00:21:41.335
- What are you talking about?
- And she's butting in,

00:21:41.335 --> 00:21:42.168
she's butting in.
- I was reiterate...

00:21:42.168 --> 00:21:43.690
Charlie was giving directions and I was--

00:21:43.690 --> 00:21:45.410
- So this is your fault?

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:47.530
- And this is why the law is hard,

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:50.960
sometimes there are just gray
areas that won't be resolved.

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:53.252
- It was Dee's fault,
she'll pay for the damages.

00:21:53.252 --> 00:21:54.085
- Don't you dare.
- Yeah,

00:21:54.085 --> 00:21:54.918
absolutely, she should.
- That's it.

00:21:54.918 --> 00:21:55.751
- Don't you dare, no.

00:21:55.751 --> 00:21:57.600
Okay, you know, I have an
idea, should we have a trial,

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:58.433
me and Charlie?

00:21:58.433 --> 00:22:00.185
- That's too many trials,
that's too many trials.

00:22:00.185 --> 00:22:01.052
- (chuckles) Oh no.
- We can't have a trial

00:22:01.052 --> 00:22:01.885
for every person.

00:22:01.885 --> 00:22:03.913
- Let's just have her pay
for it and be done with this.

00:22:03.913 --> 00:22:04.746
- Alright, that's it.

00:22:04.746 --> 00:22:05.737
- I'm tired of this.
- Let's go.

00:22:05.737 --> 00:22:06.570
- No, no, no, no.
- I'm going back to the bar.

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:08.110
- No, because Charlie was giving the...

00:22:08.110 --> 00:22:09.137
I was only...

00:22:09.137 --> 00:22:09.970
You sons of...

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:11.140
God dammit!

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:13.740
(Devin chuckles)

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:14.810
- Is that a just outcome?

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:16.440
I'll have to think about that.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:18.840
But it's a good lesson
that sometimes the law

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:21.440
is just what everyone
agrees that the law is,

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:24.670
it's just a construct based on society,

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:26.130
and sometimes you get screwed

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:28.280
because everyone else is in the majority.

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:29.113
Sorry, Dee.

00:22:30.616 --> 00:22:33.199
(upbeat music)

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:36.790
Alright, now it's time
to give this episode

00:22:36.790 --> 00:22:38.946
a grade for legal realism.

00:22:38.946 --> 00:22:41.130
(gavel bangs)

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:42.500
This was a real treat,

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:45.220
it started with one of
the classic hypotheticals

00:22:45.220 --> 00:22:46.810
in law school that you have to debate,

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:49.010
and there are reasonable
people on either side

00:22:49.010 --> 00:22:51.800
as to whether you're responsible
for consequential damages,

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:53.580
then it goes straight into something

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:55.490
that is a reasonable facsimile

00:22:55.490 --> 00:22:58.770
of arbitration between private parties,

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:02.110
and despite all the hijinks
about nonsensical issues

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:05.260
with character, and
credibility, and evolution,

00:23:05.260 --> 00:23:07.720
they actually have a
lot of good arguments,

00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:10.160
ending with an objective test

00:23:10.160 --> 00:23:12.670
to determine who is reasonably at fault.

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:15.410
This was an enormous treat
for me as a practicing lawyer

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:16.670
and someone who really enjoyed

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:19.010
all those philosophical
debates in law school.

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:21.540
I am so happy that this
episode of It's Always Sunny

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.320
was way better than the last one,

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.410
so I'm gonna give this
episode of It's Always Sunny

00:23:25.410 --> 00:23:27.030
a solid B.

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:29.800
Nice job, you're really
improving in this class.

00:23:29.800 --> 00:23:30.633
Do you agree?

00:23:30.633 --> 00:23:32.140
Leave your objections down below,

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:33.550
and check out my other reactions

00:23:33.550 --> 00:23:35.583
where I will see you in court.

